from patch_lib import Doc
d = Doc("CORE-PR-HANDOFF-v14.md")
L = d.t.split("\n")
i = [k for k, x in enumerate(L) if x.startswith("Every file named below exists in")][0]
x = L[i]
a = x.index("**Postgres (local 16.15")
tail = """**Postgres (local 16.15, not the CI version 17.10):** `pg_experiment_v14.sh`, `pg_b1.sql`, `pg_tmp.sql` (the temporary-table attack T0a and T0b, the v14 function T1, `REVOKE TEMPORARY` T2, a non-public schema T3, the trigger condition T6, the lock mode T7), `pg_m3.sql` (cost and size), `pg_real_code_v14.py` (a prototype of the new `add_spend`, not product code), `held-sql/spend_nonneg.sql`, `held-sql/spend_decrease_log.sql`, with raw output `raw-pg-version-v14.txt`, `raw-pg-b1-v14.txt`, `raw-pg-tmp-v14.txt`, `raw-pg-m3-v14.txt`, `raw-pg-real-code-v14.txt` (61 of 61 checks). The experiment log is `run-v14.log`. `gate4.sh` (the five commands of `ci.yml`) was **not run**; it was syntax-checked with `bash -n` (`raw-gate4-syntax.txt`) and compared with `ci.yml` by `check_gate4_vs_ci.py` (`raw-gate4-vs-ci.txt`). **It does not run the CI security steps (`pip-audit`, `bandit` high-only is in it, `semgrep`, `zizmor`, `gitleaks`, `trivy`; `.github/workflows/ci.yml:108-144`); those run only in CI (readiness N7).** The patch scripts that produced this file from v13 (`patch1_v14.py` to `patch7_v14.py`, helper `patch_lib.py`) are kept so every edit can be replayed. **What the citation checks do and do not show (read this).** `raw-cite-check-v14.txt`: 301 cites in the main file, 0 missing or out of range, 54 of them also checked by an anchor word. `raw-cite-check-v14-other.txt`: 90 cites, 0 missing or out of range, 18 anchor-checked. My own second program (`raw-cites-v14.txt`, 400 rows over both files): 95 with the anchor found, 199 where only the range was checked, 98 where the anchor word was not found inside the range (marked "ANCHOR-MISS"; the independent check of v13 settled 70 such rows by reading and found them all matching, and I did **not** read the 98 of v14), 7 ambiguous. So what is shown is: the files exist and the cited line ranges lie inside them. That a range holds the meaning written next to it is shown only for the anchor-checked cites and only by the word match; **a range-only cite that moved to another line in the same file is not detected by either program** (citation finding 7). The independent check of v13 matched all but three of 436 cites by reading (its findings 1, 2 and 5 are fixed here); I make no stronger claim. I did not review my own work; independent reviewers follow."""
L[i] = x[:a] + tail
d.t = "\n".join(L); d.n += 1
d.save()
